From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu
Subject: LIFE-X: cryonics

   I agree with what Perry has said here, but I think it might be
interesting to speculate on what will be needed to repair
a cryonics patient.  Is the hardest part simply developing
nanotechnology, or are there harder problems?

   For instance, is the repair technology related to the AI vision
problem? Given that a nanobot will have to "recognize" damage and repair
it, how will it go about this? If you have a nerve bundle with ~1000
connected strands, how does it know which strands to connect together
if the entire thing has been severed by fracture? To repair a damaged
cell, a nanobot may have to know what the working cell looks like,
and this might require storing and recognizing hundreds of different
cell types thoughout the body (a project on the order of the
human genome project). Or, it might be sufficient for a nanobot to
look at non-damaged cells nearby and clone them, but how do you
go about "disassembling" a cell?

  For these reasons, I think the neurosuspension option is easier
in that you don't need to worry about fixing all different kinds of
cells and organs. You can focus on brain structures instead.
Cloning a body can probably be done by biotechnology means.

  The hard part is wiring the brain in the new body. The neurosystem 
of the clone may not exactly match the brain's wiring even if the
DNA is the same. I think nerve growth largely a fractal process
and not completely encoded in the DNA. The clone may have more, or less,
nerve pathways than the original body. The brain and body
may have to "relearn" each other. If the "weights" of the neural net in the
body's nervous sysrem start off initialized, then the brain may not
be able to communicate with the nervous system -- in a sense, the new
nervous system speaks a different language.

  And what about the nervous system's information? How much of our
body's reflexes and coordination are stored outside of the brain.
If the body is similar to the subsumption architecture that cyberneticists
use, then a lot of the information that we learn, like walking,
balancing, running, grasping, etc is actually stored in the nervous
system in the limbs themselves. This might explain the superior balance
of tiger rope walkers, etc.


  I hope that these problems are easy, but as you can see, nanotech
may be the easiest part of the problem.  AI and neuropsychology may
be needed to understand more about how the nervous system and the brain
work.

  However, in terms of Pascal's Wager: I would rather gamble on
the small chance that my molecules will last long enough in cold
storage for these problems to be solved,  then gamble on the
infinitesimal chance that I will ascend from dirt and ashes
once buried.


