Date: Wed, 5 May 93 19:23 EDT
From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: CRYPTO: Steganography Tools Needed

Summary: PGP is a great tool, but it is only part of what we really need.
         We also need steganographic tools.  I propose a scheme for
         inserting hidden, encrypted messages into public plaintext
         that cannot be detected (easily) even if the eavesdropper
         knows the algorithm for inserting the hidden message.

In his introduction to PGP, Phil Zimmerman compares plaintext
messages to mail sent on postcards and encrypted messages to
mail in sealed envelopes.  Currently, using envelopes does not
arouse suspicion because almost everyone uses envelopes, but
using encryption does arouse suspicion because almost nobody
uses encryption.  Zimmerman's proposed solution is for almost
everyone to routinely use encryption, so that encrypted
messages will be the norm.

I do not believe that this will succeed, at least not in the
way Zimmerman hopes.  Even though PGP is freeware and fairly
readily accessible, no "solidarity" of PGP users will arise
unless email with PGP encryption becomes transparently convenient
to use and also does not invite lawsuits from PKP (Public Key Partners,
owners of the RSA-related patents) or intimidation from government
agencies.  The kinds of encryption that *will* become readily
available, easy-to-use, and legally hassle-free will be the kinds
that our governments want us to have.  (Note that even hardware
vendors, such as Apple, have been encouraged to cripple their
encryption, even though I am sure that PKP would gladly make some
money by licensing its uncrippled algorithms to them.  Instead,
we are getting crippled encryption such as the Clipper/Capstone
Wiretap chips.)

Given the actual, rather than professed, intent of governments,
this is to be expected.  Also, we shouldn't have ever depended on
any "solidarity" of email users to secure our own privacy in the
first place.  It is much better to take the matter in our own hands
than to try to get large numbers of people to help us out.  By using
sufficiently intelligent steganographic techniques, we will not
need any "solidarity" from other people at all.  We will make our
"envelopes" look like "postcards," and thus not arouse any suspicion.
Then we can not only send messages that only the intended receiver
can read, but also send them so that only the intended receiver
can determine that there is even a hidden message to be read.

What steganographic techniques might be sufficient?  Certainly,
hiding a few bits in a grainy black & white image should not
be too difficult, provided the image is not compressed with a
lossy compression algorithm.  But most of our email traffic is
text.  It would be best to have good steganographic techniques
for normal English prose.

To give you something to pick at, here is my proposal.  I expect
that people more familiar with cryptanalytic techniques will be
able to do better.  In particular, since my system is quite simple,
I wouldn't be surprised if something like it has already been
thoroughly studied.  If so, I would like to see what has been said
about it.

Proposed Steganographic System

A steganographic system requires:

  (1) a public encryption key for the intended receiver of the
      hidden message,
  (2) the (compressed & encrypted) private message,
  (3) the public text,
  (4) rules for generating variations on the public text, and
  (5) parameters describing the insertion of the private message
      into the public message.

Parts (1) and (2) are already solved by PGP and other public key
cryptosystems.  Part (3) requires a prolific writer, since posting
two or more variations of the same public text to convey more than
one hidden message would expose its use for steganography.  (It will
be tempting to use a story-writing program to create the public text.
Most such programs produce suspiciously artificial-looking text,
though, which would expose the file as a likely source of a hidden
message.)  Part (4) is the hardest and Part (5) is a catchall for
the parameters used in Part (4).

To start simple, let one bit of the private message (2) be inserted
each N bits of the public message (3&4), where N is one of the
parameters in (5).  This bit could be the XOR of those N bits (i.e.
the parity).  The trick is to arrange the public text so that its
parity is correct for every block of N bits.  How could that be done?
By using transformational grammars.  For example, the sentence:
    "But most of our email traffic is text."
can be rewritten:
    "But our email traffic is mostly text."
or:
    "But text is most of our email traffic."
or:
    "But mostly our email traffic is text."
Hopefully, enough alternatives can be generated automatically to produce
the desired parity bits, provided N is chosen well.  (Is a good database
of transformational grammar rules readily available?)  Furthermore, when
the transformational grammar does not give sufficient flexibility, an
on-line thesaurus may be used for synonym substitution.

To send a private message using this system, do the following:

  (1) compress & encrypt the private message using the public key
      of the intended receiver,
  (2) write or find a public plaintext, of sufficient length,
      that has not been used before,
  (3) pick a value for N, the insertion interval,
  (4) run a program to transform the public text until the parity of
      each N bit block matches the bits of the compressed/encrypted
      private message, and
  (5) email or post to a news group the resulting text.

When the intended receiver reads the file, he or she does the following:

  for all reasonable values of N
    compute the parity for each N bit block
    attempt to decrypt and decompress the result, using the
      private decryption key
    if decryption succeeds, exit the loop successfully

When someone other than the intended receiver (the eavesdropper) reads
the file, the algorithm above will always lead to failure, because nobody
but the intended receiver has the private decryption key.  The
eavesdropper's conclusion can only be that either no hidden message
exists or that a hidden message exists that is undetectable.  Unless
the eavesdropper is clever.

Well-encrypted files may be too uniformly random.  For example, their
bits may have a different standard deviation than the parity bits of
blocks of normal English text.  If so, then a simple statistical analysis
of the plaintext can reveal a likely hidden message, although not be able
to decrypt it.  If so, then this technique will need to be fine-tuned
so that the existence of a hidden message can be better hidden.

Please let me know if you have statistics on the distribution of
parity bits of blocks of normal English text.  (If not, maybe I'll
have to do it myself.)  Also, if you know of a good database of
transformational grammar rules for English, please let me know.
Thanks.
