From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: DAISY: the death of Hal 9000

Simon Kinahan writes:
>
>> Elias Israel writes:
>

(Actually, that's me you're quoting and not Elias)

>> I hate to inject a note of skepticism, but these sorts of cutesy analogies are
>> rampant when neural net research is popularized.  You have to be careful in
>> drawing such correlations as support for a particular theory of consciousness
>> or cognitive implementation.
>
>  Hmmm, I disagree. One of the claims about NNs is that they are more like human 
>minds than 'conventional AI' (whatever that is).
>  The fact that they make mistakes which seem human gives a certain amount of
>supporting evdence to this claim.

I don't think it gives very much evidence at all.  NN's are not _closer_ to
real minds than AI systems, they're _less_unlike_.  While things like
parallel architecture, distributed encoding, pattern matching go on in NN's
and probably also in the brain, most current models are really quite far from
the biology.  The most popular NN system, backpropagation, makes such
unrealistic (from a biological perspective) assumptions that few psychologists
would regard its results very highly.  I'd guess that most of the claims you've
heard refer to backprop, and IMHO it's not much in terms of a brain model.

I believe that most of the confusion on this issue comes from the suggestive
language applied to neural networks.  When an NN gives the correct response we
call that "recall", when it doesn't we say it "forgets"  (When it recalls
something never presented we say "deja-vu").  A bystander will be impressed by
this and go home with images of disembodied silicon minds (and most researchers
don't mind this, it gives them mystique and funding).  But if you dig deep past
the fancy jargon you see how far we really are from understanding what goes on
in biological, especially human, brains.  But give us more funding and someday
we will come up with something...  :-]

-Paul


